				"Conifers first appear in the fossil record over 300 million years ago in the [[Carboniferous]]. They became dominant land plants in the [[Mesozoic]], until flowering plants took over many ecosystems in the [[Cretaceous]]. Many conifers today are relict species, surviving in a small part of their former ranges. Such relicts include Wollemia, known only from a small area of Australia, and Metasequoia glyptostroboides, known from Cretaceous fossils and surviving in a small area of China. Although the total number of species is relatively small, conifers are [[Ecology|ecologically]] important. They are the dominant plants over the [[Taiga|taiga]] of the [[Northern Hemisphere]]. Boreal conifers have multiple adaptations to survive winters, including a conical shape to shed snow, strong tracheid vessels to tolerate ice pressure, and a waxy covering on the needle leaves to minimize water loss.",
