		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Dietary fiber consists of non-[[Starch|starch]] [[Polysaccharide|polysaccharides]] and other plant components, such as [[Cellulose|cellulose]], resistant starch, resistant dextrins, inulins, lignins, [[Chitin|chitins]], pectins, beta-glucans, and oligosaccharides. Food sources of dietary fiber have traditionally been divided according to proportion of soluble vs. insoluble fiber. The two types are defined according to their characteristics of viscosity and fermentability, with different advantages between the types. Plant foods contain both types in varying amounts. Bulking fibers, such as cellulose and hemicellulose (including psyllium), absorb and hold water, promoting bowel movement regularity. Viscous fibers, such as beta-glucan and psyllium, thicken the fecal mass.",
